What follows is a list of all the plugins I use, sorted into specific usecases and a short explaination.


## Note-Taking
### Editor feel
<PluginRack group="editor-feel" />

All to meet my needs of navigating quickly. I grew dependent the Vim keybindings. 

My `.obsidian.vimrc`:
```
" Have j and k navigate visual lines
nnoremap j gj
nnoremap k gk
nnoremap gj j
nnoremap gk k

" Quickly remove search highlights with ESC
nmap <ESC> :nohl<CR>

"" Smarter o and O (inserting prefix for markdown lists). 
nmap o A<CR>
nmap O kA<CR>

" Yank to system clipboard
set clipboard=unnamed
```

### Math and code
<PluginRack group="math-and-code" />
Quick Latex is probably the most important plugin of them all. $\LaTeX$ equations get rapid fast and my custom shorthand list grew to almost $90$. 
VSCode Editor is nice if you need to look something not .md up quick.

### Sketches and diagrams
<PluginRack group="sketches-and-diagrams" />
Excalidraw should be self-explaining, as one of the worlds most popular whiteboards.

I'm a bit unhappy with the drawing & config speed though, Diagrams are clunky. This is where Mermaid comes into play. Obsidian has a builtin mermaid renderer, just label a block code with `mermaid`. I still struggle with the syntax though, thus I use the toolbar.

A small regret I have is Obsidian Functionplot.  Have used it a few times and am now stuck with the plugin for backwards compatibility. *Don't be like me, actually think about what you'll use*.

### Sources
<PluginRack group="getting-sources-in" />
Both PDF++ and Media Extended are in my daily workflow. I usually splitscreen the source and my notes.

PDF++ is especially good, because it supports dynamic embeds. It just specifies a rectangle in the source file and renders on-demand, no additional images sitting in assets.
Media Extended sadly just takes screenshots. I'm not even sure if dynamic loading would be resourceful though, no idea if the .mp4 file structure would support this.

### Consistency
<PluginRack group="keeping-the-text-clean" />

Linter is great for solving a very specific problem of mine, because I often use this note structure:

```
**Technical term**
- Aspect 1
- Aspect 2
**Broken term (gets wrapped upwards)**
- Aspect 1
``` 

Sadly, lines directly after lists in markdown gets merged into the previous line. 
I forced an empty line after every bullet list with this custom regex:

`^((?:[ \t]*[-*+] .*\n)+)(?=[ \t]*(?![-*+] )\S)` - `gm` - `$1\n`

I won't explain it though, go figure it out yourself at [Regex101](https://regex101.com/?regex=%5E((%3F%3A%5B%20%5Ct%5D*%5B-*%2B%5D%20.*%5Cn)%2B)(%3F%3D%5B%20%5Ct%5D*(%3F!%5B-*%2B%5D%20)%5CS)&testString=**Technical%20term**%0A-%20Aspect%201%0A-%20Aspect%202%0A**Broken%20term%20(gets%20wrapped%20upwards)**%0A-%20Aspect%201%0A&flags=gm&flavor=javascript&delimiter=%2F) — the example above is already loaded as the test string.

## Visuals

### Styling
<PluginRack group="how-a-note-looks" />

I'm currently using the [Soft Paper](https://github.com/nickmilo/soft-paper) Theme, mainly for its customisability with style settings. I also like the pastel background.

### How the screen looks
<PluginRack group="a-quiet-window" />

I found the standard view to be much to cluttered and distracting, thus I hid most of it. I hid:
- The ribbon
- the tab title bar
- Many Statusbar items, including Vim mode
- backlink, canvas, footnotes, page-preview, daily-notes, slash-command, bookmarks, markdown-importer, zk-prefixer, random-note, audio-recorder, publish, bases

I also have keyboard shortcuts to directly hide sidebars and tabbar. This means in fullscreen I only see my note, perfect focus.


