## Navigation & Search
<PluginRack group="finding-my-way-around" />

Multiple Notes outline is a gem I found relatively recently.
I configured it to display the outline of every note in a course folder, superb for navigation and getting a overview on how far your notes are done.

## Getting things out of the vault
<PluginRack group="getting-things-out" />

## Service callers
<PluginRack group="wired-to-the-outside" />
Even though claude integration is very easy (see above), I like to have a custom view for the LLM. Claudian gives me that: it embeds the agent in a sidebar pane with the vault as its working directory, and it renders $\LaTeX$, important for math questions. I've tried many agent plugins, Claudian was the best.

Custom system prompt:
> You are mainly used as a university tutor for students with solid CS experience (especially ML). Act accordingly, highlight connections and explain with related concepts.
> If you create a file, mark it with the "LLM: Claude" property.
> If you modify a file, insert "--- (Claude)" as header and footer to your modification.

## Under the hood
<PluginRack group="under-the-hood" />

Custom Attachment Location is very important to me, since my hierarchical filestructure needs me to have embeds near the actual note. This is where I store my embeds:

```
.
├── assets/
│   ├── Note1/
│   │   └── img1.png
│   └── Note2/
│       └── drawing.excalidraw
├── Note1.md (embeds img1.png)
└── Note2.md (embeds drawing.excalidraw)
```


## Sync and backup

I actually don't have a dedicated backup system for obsidian. I put all of my documents in a partition **BEFORE** my OS. This is backed up to Google drive using [this](https://support.google.com/a/users/answer/13022292?hl=en).

## Disclaimer
Keep in mind that nothing good comes from optimising your note-taking system to the brim, it's just wasted time. If another system works for you, honestly keep it. But if you're already deep in the obsidian rabbithole, I suggest giving my method a try :). Thanks for reading my second blogpost!